# Source: HoareLogic Exercises.pdf ("ICT3101 Exercises, Deductive Verification"), problem 3 b), with its solution in "HoareLogic Exercises Solutions - problems 1 - 3.pdf".
id: fill-precondition-false
title: Find the precondition when the postcondition cannot be reached
topic: Sequential composition
level: 2
source: HoareLogic Exercises, problem 3 b
tags: assignment, sequence, wp, false

task:
  Find the weakest precondition of the program for the postcondition $Q$.
  Calculate it statement by statement from the last one. The variables
  are integers.

  The question only asks for the precondition. To have a complete triple,
  the exercise uses the precondition $P$: $false$. At the end, the
  consequence rule checks this precondition.

pre: false
post: x > z && y = 3

program:
  {P}
  {R3}
  z := x + 2;      // i1
  {R2}
  y := z + z;      // i2
  {R1}
  x := z + y       // i3
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: R1, the weakest precondition of x := z + y
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i3
text:
  The precondition of an assignment is obtained by substituting the
  assigned variable ($x$) with the assignment expression ($z + y$) in the
  postcondition $Q$.
calc:
  wp(x := z + y, Q)
  = (x > z && y = 3)[x <- z + y]
  = z + y > z && y = 3     // the x of Q is replaced by z + y
  = y > 0 && y = 3         // subtract z from both sides of z + y > z
  = y = 3                  // y = 3 already implies y > 0
set: R1 = y = 3

step: R2, the weakest precondition of y := z + z
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i2
text:
  We substitute the assigned variable ($y$) with the assignment expression
  ($z + z$) in the postcondition of this statement, which is $R1$.
calc:
  wp(y := z + z, R1)
  = (y = 3)[y <- z + z]
  = z + z = 3              // the y of R1 is replaced by z + z
  = 2 * z = 3              // z + z is 2 * z
  = false                  // 2 * z is even and 3 is odd, so no integer z satisfies it
set: R2 = false

step: R3, the weakest precondition of z := x + 2
rule: ASSIGN, WP
focus: i1
text:
  We substitute the assigned variable ($z$) with the assignment expression
  ($x + 2$) in the postcondition of this statement, which is $R2$.
calc:
  wp(z := x + 2, R2)
  = false[z <- x + 2]
  = false                  // z does not occur in false, so nothing changes
set: R3 = false

step: The precondition must imply the weakest precondition
rule: CONSEQUENCE
text:
  $R3$ is the weakest precondition of the whole program for $Q$. It is
  $false$: no initial state makes the program end in $Q$. By the
  consequence rule, the triple is valid when the precondition $P$ implies
  $R3$.
  >>> «false : P» ==> «false : R3»
vc: false ==> false
why:
  $false$ implies any formula. The triple is valid, but it says nothing
  about the program, because no state satisfies $P$.
result: The weakest precondition is $false$. The triple with the precondition $false$ is valid, but only trivially.

summary:
  At the end $y = z + z$, which is even, so it can never be $3$. The
  calculation finds this without running the program: the conjunct
  $y = 3$ of $Q$ becomes $z + z = 3$.

  If the variables were rational numbers, $z + z = 3$ would give
  $z = 3/2$, and the weakest precondition would be $x = -1/2$. Over the
  integers there is no solution.

  The tests of this exercise are not conclusive on the specification: no
  state satisfies the precondition $false$, so there is nothing to run.
